          deepDive: "**AにしろBにしろ** means *whether A or B / be it A or B*. The book gives two uses: (1) pick examples from a group and say the statement holds **not only for these but for all of them** — 東京にしろ大阪にしろ大都市には働く場所が多い (*big cities, whether Tokyo or Osaka, have lots of jobs*); and (2) with **opposites** (いい・悪い, 好き・嫌い, する・しない, 出席する・欠席する), say it holds **either way** — 出席するにせよ、欠席するにせよ、期日までに返事を出す (*whether you attend or not, reply by the deadline*). にしろ is にする + the old imperative しろ: *make it A, make it B — it's the same*.\n\nForms (as in the book): plain form + にしろ／にしても／にせよ, repeated; なA and N drop だ (好きにしろ、嫌いにしろ; 理系にしても文系にしても), and formal である is also possible. Register: **にせよ** is the most formal and literary, **にしろ** is written–neutral, and **にしても** is the most conversational. The meaning is the same, and one pair normally keeps the same form.\n\nWith a **question word** (📎 note), one にしろ is enough: どこに行くにしろ, どうなるにせよ, and the set phrase **いずれにしろ／いずれにしても／いずれにせよ** (*either way, in any case*).\n\nConstraints: both items come from the same category (dogs and cats, the sciences and the humanities), and the second half is something that holds regardless — a judgment, obligation, advice, rule or general truth: 〜なければならない, 〜のが礼儀だ, 〜なさい, 〜はだめだ. It isn't used to report a single past event.\n\nContrasts:\n- **〜やら〜やら** (#98): lists examples to say *there are all sorts of things*: 犬やら猫やら、様々なペットを飼っている. There's no *either way* meaning. The practice contrasts it with 犬にしろ猫にしろ、ペットは飼えない (*no pets, whether dogs or cats*).\n- **〜につけ** (#94): *every time ~ (a feeling arises)*: 写真を見るにつけ懐かしく思い出す — repeated emotion, not \"regardless\".\n- **〜にかかわらず** (#5) / **〜を問わず** (#2): *regardless of* — stiffer, used with a noun covering a range or a pair (天候にかかわらず, 出席するしないにかかわらず). にしろ names the two cases and often leads to advice.\n- A single 〜にしても after one clause can also mean *even granted that ~*: 忙しいにしても、電話ぐらいできるだろう.\n\nCommon mistakes: ✗好きだにしろ → ✓好きにしろ; ✗何を買うにつけ → ✓何を買うにしろ (*whatever you buy*).\n\nJLPT tip (まとめ 問題1-2): 食べ物を売る店は、どんな店（にしろ）衛生管理をきちんとしなければならない — どんな + an obligation → にしろ. にしては means *for (surprisingly)*, につけ needs a recurring feeling, and ばかりか means *not only*. The listening uses it too: 野球選手にしろ、教師にしろ、自分の人生だ (*baseball player or teacher, it's his own life*).",
